# Surface: nushell — the daily driver.
# Schema, verify kinds and writing rules: see README.md in this directory.
# Entries are grouped by topic in the order topics.nuon declares.
#
# These entries describe the shipped configuration. There is no checker: an
# entry is true because someone deployed the change and used it.
[
    # ---------------------------------------------------------------- navigate
    {
        cmd: "z <query>"
        title: "Jump to a directory by a fragment of its path"
        use: "Type `z` and any part of the path — `z dot` reaches ~/dev/dotfiles. Zoxide picks the best frecency match and you land there; the listing prints itself. A single argument that resolves to an existing *file* opens it in nvim instead of jumping, so `z notes.md` edits."
        topic: "navigate"
        task: "directories"
        step: 2
        mode: "shell"
        also: ["zi", "zz", "<word>"]
        why: "Every jump goes through the `cd` funnel, so the start dir, the dirstack and the recents log update no matter which route you took. A jump that does not move PWD logs nothing — a failed `z` leaves no trace."
    }
    {
        cmd: "zi"
        title: "Pick a directory from zoxide interactively"
        use: "Run `zi` with no argument and the zoxide database opens in an fzf picker, ordered by frecency: type to narrow it, then `Enter` on a row and you land there. The picker is fzf, not television — it shells out to `zoxide query --interactive` — and it is the one accepted exception to tv owning every picker screen."
        topic: "navigate"
        task: "directories"
        step: 3
        mode: "shell"
        also: ["z <query>"]
        why: "The exception was granted rather than tolerated: drawing this list from a cable file would mean reimplementing zoxide's frecency ranking inside television, and that ranking is the only reason the list is worth opening. So fzf sits in the required package set as zoxide's dependency, never as a picker anything else may reach for — a second picker outside tv is a new decision, not an appeal to this one. Decided 2026-08-21 (user)."
    }
    {
        cmd: "zz"
        title: "Toggle back to the directory you came from"
        use: "Run `zz` — it is `cd -`. Run it again to come back. It pairs with the bare-word jump: word, look, `zz`."
        topic: "navigate"
        task: "directories"
        step: 4
        mode: "shell"
        also: ["<word>"]
    }
    {
        cmd: "<word>"
        title: "Jump by typing a directory name on its own"
        use: "Type a bare word that is not a command — `dotfiles` — and press enter. If zoxide knows a directory matching it you land there with a cleared screen; the fresh prompt in the new directory is the confirmation. If it knows nothing, you get the ordinary command-not-found."
        topic: "navigate"
        task: "directories"
        step: 1
        mode: "shell"
        also: ["z <query>", "zz"]
        why: "The jump happens in `pre_execution` because that is the only hook where a cd persists, and the screen is cleared in `pre_prompt` to bury the command-not-found error that is already on its way. The trigger is deliberately narrow — first token unknown, no shell metacharacters, not path-shaped — so `./x` and `ls | nope` never jump. It queries `zoxide query --exclude $PWD` directly and only jumps on a real hit, because the funnel reads an empty argument as no argument at all and would otherwise take you HOME on every miss."
    }
    {
        cmd: "cd <path>"
        title: "Enter a directory, creating it if it does not exist"
        use: "Point `cd` at a path that is not there and it offers to make it: press `Enter` to confirm and you are in the new directory. Any key that is not blank prints `aborted` and cancels, so the reflex `y` is a cancel — while `Space` and `Tab` confirm, because the check is for a non-blank keypress rather than for `Enter`. `cd` alone goes home, `cd -` toggles back. Everything else about it is the builtin."
        topic: "navigate"
        task: "directories"
        step: 7
        mode: "shell"
        also: ["mkcd", "z <query>"]
        why: "`cd` is aliased to the `mkcd` wrapper, which is the single funnel every kind of navigation reaches the shell through."
    }
    {
        cmd: "mkcd"
        title: "Understand the one funnel every move goes through"
        use: "Nothing to run — this is the shape of the shell. Real `cd`, zoxide jumps, picker jumps and the bare-word fallback all arrive at `mkcd`, and the PWD hook is the single place that reacts to a move."
        topic: "navigate"
        task: "directories"
        step: 8
        kind: "concept"
        mode: "shell"
        also: ["cd <path>", "<word>", "Ctrl-Q"]
        why: "Because there is exactly one funnel and exactly one reaction point, four things stay true however you moved: the directory is recorded to `startdir.txt` so a new pane opens where you left off, it is pushed onto the dirstack that feeds the recent-dirs picker, the pick is logged to the recents list, and the listing prints. Scatter that per navigation command and one route always ends up forgetting one of the four."
        # `prose` was wrong here and hid a real handle: this entry describes a
        # concept, but `mkcd` is a command you can type and introspection can
        # see. A concept entry over a real handle should name it.
    }
    {
        cmd: "ls"
        title: "List a directory as structured, decorated data"
        use: "Plain `ls`. Rows are sorted by type then modified time, so directories group together and the freshest thing sits nearest the prompt, and every row except a directory carries a Nerd Font icon — one for its extension, or a generic one where the extension is unknown or unmapped; directory rows show none. It is still a nushell table: `ls | where size > 1mb | sort-by modified` works."
        topic: "navigate"
        task: "listing"
        step: 1
        mode: "shell"
        also: ["l / ll / la", "ls --du"]
        why: "The builtin is captured as `core-ls` before being shadowed, because an alias target binds at parse time — without the capture the wrapper would call itself."
    }
    {
        cmd: "ls --du"
        title: "List with real recursive directory sizes"
        use: "Add `-d`/`--du` to swap each directory's inode size for what it actually occupies on disk — the builtin's own flag; this wrapper only adds the icon column on top of it."
        topic: "navigate"
        task: "listing"
        step: 3
        mode: "shell"
        also: ["ls"]
        why: "Opt-in only, and it stays that way: a single `node_modules` would stall every listing in the tree if this were the default."
    }
    {
        cmd: "l / ll / la"
        title: "List short, long, or including dotfiles"
        use: "`l` is the quick one, `ll` the long form, `la` includes hidden files. `la` is also what runs by itself after every directory change."
        topic: "navigate"
        task: "listing"
        step: 2
        mode: "shell"
        also: ["ls"]
    }
    {
        cmd: "y"
        title: "Open the yazi file manager"
        use: "`y` is `yazi`, the terminal file manager: move with the arrows or `hjkl`, `Enter` opens a file or descends into a directory, `q` quits back to the shell."
        topic: "navigate"
        task: "listing"
        step: 4
        mode: "shell"
    }

    # -------------------------------------------------------------------- find
    {
        key: "Ctrl-Q"
        title: "Reopen something you picked or jumped to recently"
        use: "Press `Ctrl-Q` for one list of everything recent across every channel — jumps, files opened via `z`, finder picks — newest first. `enter` opens it by type. `ctrl-r` replays instead: it cds to the directory the pick was made in and re-runs the channel it came from, so a grep result reopens the same search where you ran it. An empty log prints a hint rather than an empty picker."
        topic: "find"
        task: "history"
        step: 5
        mode: "shell"
        also: ["F3", "mkcd"]
        # Two handles, one gesture: the key, and the command it runs. Naming
        # only the key left `quicklist` reported undocumented in the reverse
        # direction while the entry describing it sat right here.
    }
    {
        cmd: "finder"
        title: "Run a picker and get typed nushell data back"
        use: "`finder` opens the channel remote; `finder --start files` opens one channel directly. The return value is data, not a string: a list of paths, `{file, line, text}` rows from a grep, `{hash}` records from `git-log`. Pipe it like anything else."
        topic: "find"
        task: "files"
        step: 3
        mode: "shell"
        also: ["F3", "tv channel"]
        why: "Typed decode per channel is what lets one opener do the right thing for a file, a directory, a grep hit and a commit — and it is why new pickers are new channels plus a decode, never a new hand-rolled TUI."
    }
    {
        cmd: "tv channel"
        title: "Understand what a channel is, and add one"
        use: "Nothing to run. A channel is a cable file under `~/.config/television` naming a source command that emits rows, plus how to preview one. Add a file and `tv <name>` and `finder --start <name>` reach it immediately; teach `finder` how to decode its rows and every opener works on it too. `F3` is the one thing it does NOT join for free — the search-all corpus is a fixed list of lanes in `tv-all`, because a merged row is only useful if something knows what to do when you pick it, so a new channel worth searching from F3 is a lane and an opener added there."
        topic: "find"
        task: "files"
        step: 5
        kind: "concept"
        mode: "shell"
        also: ["finder", "F3"]
        why: "One rule keeps the picker surface from sprawling: television owns every picker screen. The curated set is files, dirs, text, zoxide, env, quicklist, git-log, git-files, git-branch, recent dirs (`recent-dirs`, fed by the dirstack), recent files, alias, `nu-history` and `docs` (every line of the manual's prose, opened in the editor at the hit), plus the `theme` channel behind the scheme picker — anything else migrates on demand. The rule has exactly one exception, and it is named rather than tolerated: `zi` reaches fzf through `zoxide query --interactive`. A second picker outside tv would be a new decision, not an appeal to that one. tv runs on the `default` ANSI theme so it inherits the terminal's palette instead of baking hex values."
    }
    {
        cmd: "grep"
        title: "Search file contents with ripgrep"
        use: "`grep <pattern>` is `rg <pattern>` — recursive by default, respecting ignore files. Reach for `rg` and `fd`, not `grep` and `find`."
        topic: "find"
        task: "files"
        step: 4
        mode: "shell"
        also: ["F3", "idioms"]
        why: "`rg` and `fd` share one ignore ruleset through `RIPGREP_CONFIG_PATH`, so a path hidden from one is hidden from both."
    }

    # ----------------------------------------------------------------- history
    {
        key: "Ctrl-R"
        title: "Search this directory's history"
        use: "Press `Ctrl-R`: a picker opens over the commands you have run *in this directory*, newest first and deduplicated, prefilled with whatever you had typed up to the cursor. Enter replaces the command line with the pick; Esc leaves the line alone."
        topic: "history"
        task: "history"
        step: 1
        mode: "shell"
        also: ["Alt-R", "Up / Down"]
        why: "Directory-scoped is the default because a command worth repeating is nearly always one you ran here; the global list is one modifier away. The sqlite store runs with `isolation: false` so every pane contributes to one merged history."
    }
    {
        key: "Alt-R"
        title: "Search the whole history, from anywhere"
        use: "Press `Alt-R` for television's own shell-history picker over every command from every directory. Enter puts the pick on the command line."
        topic: "history"
        task: "history"
        step: 2
        mode: "shell"
        also: ["Ctrl-R", "Shift+Up / Shift+Down"]
        why: "`Alt`, not `Ctrl-Shift`. The kitty keyboard protocol stays off — it leaks `^[[?0u` through the WezTerm pty — and without it shift is indistinguishable on a control+letter chord, so `Ctrl-Shift-R` would arrive as plain `Ctrl-R` and shadow the local picker."
    }
    {
        key: "Up / Down"
        title: "Step through this directory's history on the line"
        use: "With the cursor on the command line, `Up` walks back through this directory's commands and `Down` forward; typing anything resets you to the newest. No picker, no screen change."
        topic: "history"
        task: "history"
        step: 3
        mode: "shell"
        also: ["Shift+Up / Shift+Down", "Ctrl-R"]
        why: "Both keys try `menuup`/`menudown` first in an `until` chain, so with a completion menu open the arrows still navigate the menu. And these bindings are appended *after* television's generated init, because reedline resolves last-entry-wins and tv's own Ctrl-R would otherwise take the key."
    }
    {
        key: "Shift+Up / Shift+Down"
        title: "Step through the whole history on the line"
        use: "Hold shift with the arrows for reedline's own global traversal — the same inline stepping, ignoring which directory you are in. Use it when the command you want was run somewhere else."
        topic: "history"
        task: "history"
        step: 4
        mode: "shell"
        also: ["Up / Down", "Alt-R"]
    }

    # -------------------------------------------------------------------- edit
    {
        key: "Esc"
        title: "Clear the command line"
        use: "Press `Esc` and the command line clears, the way `Ctrl-C` would. It is an unconditional clear with no menu-close branch, so the line goes whether or not a completion or history menu is open — this is not a two-stage escape."
        topic: "edit"
        task: "editing"
        step: 4
        mode: "shell"
    }
    {
        cmd: "cat <file>"
        title: "Dump a file with syntax highlighting and no pager"
        use: "`cat <file>` is `bat --paging=never`: highlighted, line-numbered, and it still pipes like `cat` because nothing waits for a key."
        topic: "edit"
        task: "editing"
        step: 3
        mode: "shell"
    }
    {
        cmd: "nv / vi"
        title: "Open Neovim"
        use: "Either name starts nvim. `$EDITOR` and `$VISUAL` are nvim too, so anything that opens an editor for you — the finder, `z` on a file, a commit message — lands in the same place."
        topic: "edit"
        task: "editing"
        step: 1
        mode: "shell"
        also: ["nn"]
    }
    {
        cmd: "nn"
        title: "Open the notes file"
        use: "`nn` opens `~/notes.md` in nvim, from wherever you are."
        topic: "edit"
        task: "editing"
        step: 2
        mode: "shell"
        also: ["nv / vi"]
    }
    {
        cmd: "cf <file>"
        title: "Copy a file's contents to the system clipboard"
        use: "`cf <file>` puts the whole file on the clipboard. The argument is `path`-typed, so Tab completes it. A missing file errors instead of copying nothing."
        topic: "edit"
        task: "copy"
        step: 4
        mode: "shell"
        why: "It picks `pbcopy`, `wl-copy` or `xclip` by session type and checks the display variables first, so on a headless box it fails fast rather than hanging on a clipboard nobody is listening to."
    }

    # --------------------------------------------------------------------- git
    {
        cmd: "g"
        title: "Run git"
        use: "`g` is `git`: `g status`, `g push`, everything as usual, three characters shorter."
        topic: "git"
        task: "git"
        step: 1
        mode: "shell"
        also: ["lg"]
    }
    {
        cmd: "lg"
        title: "Open lazygit on this repository"
        use: "`lg` in any repo: staging, hunk-level commits, rebases and the log in one terminal UI. Quit with `q`."
        topic: "git"
        task: "git"
        step: 2
        mode: "shell"
        also: ["g"]
    }

    # ---------------------------------------------------------------- terminal
    {
        cmd: "q / :q / /exit"
        title: "Leave the shell"
        use: "Any of the three exits. They exist because muscle memory arrives from vim, from a REPL, and from a chat box, and none of them should print an error."
        topic: "terminal"
        task: "windows"
        step: 7
        mode: "shell"
    }

    # ------------------------------------------------------------------ agents
    {
        cmd: "cc [...args]"
        title: "Start Claude in this directory"
        use: "`cc` launches Claude Code with permission prompts skipped, in the directory you are in. Arguments pass straight through."
        topic: "agents"
        task: "agents"
        step: 1
        mode: "shell"
        also: ["cr [...args]", "credentials in a capsule"]
        why: "With a single login profile it starts immediately — no picker. The multi-login machinery only wakes up once a second profile exists, so the common case pays nothing for it."
    }
    {
        cmd: "cr [...args]"
        title: "Resume a Claude session in this directory"
        use: "`cr` is `cc --resume`: it offers the sessions from this directory and continues the one you pick. Given an id it resumes that one without asking, which is the form `F4 s` types for you."
        topic: "agents"
        task: "agents"
        step: 2
        does: "resume-claude"
        mode: "shell"
        also: ["cc [...args]", "F4 s"]
    }
    {
        cmd: "help --json"
        title: "Read this manual as structured data"
        use: "`help --json` emits the whole manual — topics and every entry field — as one JSON document, for an agent that would rather parse than read. Plain `help` under capture is already unstyled text, so this is the optimisation, not the requirement."
        topic: "agents"
        task: "agents"
        step: 8
        mode: "shell"
        also: ["help", "idioms"]
        why: "The field names are an interface: renaming one is a breaking change and has to be recorded in the agent-interface PRD."
    }
    {
        cmd: "idioms"
        title: "Search with rg, find with fd, pick with tv"
        use: "Nothing to run — four rules that stop an agent guessing. Search with `rg` and find with `fd`, never `grep`/`find`. Pick with television (`tv`) — the single exception is `zi`, which opens fzf, and it is the only place fzf belongs. Nushell commands return structured data, so filter with `| where`, not `| grep`. And `cd` here can create the directory it is given."
        topic: "agents"
        task: "agents"
        step: 7
        kind: "concept"
        mode: "shell"
        also: ["grep", "tv channel", "cd <path>", "zi"]
        why: "Every one of these is a failure this environment has actually seen: reaching for a tool that is not installed, or piping a table into a line-oriented filter and getting nothing."
    }

    # ------------------------------------------------------------------ config
    {
        cmd: "help"
        title: "Read the manual for this environment"
        use: "Run `help` alone for the topics and the handful of keys worth knowing first. `help <topic>` gives that topic as a nushell table, so `help find | where key =~ ctrl` composes; `help <entry>` gives one entry in full, with its why. A name this manual does not document but the shell does is handed to nushell's own `std/help` untouched, which is what keeps every `<cmd> --help` working. Whatever is left is searched across the manual, and if that finds nothing the search falls through to `std/help`'s own, so `help <nu-word>` still lands somewhere."
        topic: "config"
        task: "maintaining"
        step: 1
        mode: "shell"
        also: ["?", "help --json"]
        why: "Nushell sanctions overriding `help`, which is the constraint the delegation exists for: this command also receives every `<anything> --help` typed in the shell, so it cannot be a manual that happens to be called `help`. Where a name is BOTH a nushell command and an entry here — `ls` is the live example — the manual wins; `core-help ls` is the alias that reaches nushell's own, and it is the only way to, because the flags that used to address either side explicitly are gone."
    }
    {
        cmd: "docs"
        title: "Grep the manual's prose and open it in the editor"
        use: "`docs` — or `?`, which is the same command — opens a picker over every LINE of this manual's markdown: the guide, the reference, and the internals notes that hold the constraints the configuration is built around. Type a phrase you half-remember (`OSC 52`, `parse time`, `capsule`); enter opens that file in `$EDITOR` at that line. `help` addresses entries by name, `docs` addresses sentences."
        topic: "config"
        task: "maintaining"
        step: 2
        does: "search-manual"
        mode: "shell"
        also: ["?", "help"]
        why: "The manual was a documentation site that had to be built and served before it could be read, which meant it was not read. Markdown under `~/.config/nushell/help/manual`, `rg` and one television channel are the same search with nothing to start — and the file it lands you in is the file you edit."
    }
    {
        cmd: "?"
        title: "Grep the manual's prose and open it in the editor"
        use: "`?` is `docs` in one keystroke: the picker over every line of the manual."
        topic: "config"
        task: "maintaining"
        step: 2
        does: "search-manual"
        mode: "shell"
        also: ["docs"]
    }
    {
        cmd: "rr"
        title: "Pull and apply the latest dotfiles"
        use: "`rr` is `chezmoi update --force`: fetch the dotfiles repo and apply it over `~`. Applying twice changes nothing the second time."
        topic: "config"
        task: "maintaining"
        step: 5
        mode: "shell"
    }
    {
        cmd: "pass <tab>"
        title: "Complete pass subcommands and entry names"
        use: "Type `pass ` and press Tab: the verbs come up, and so do the live entry names from `$PASSWORD_STORE_DIR`, without their `.gpg` suffix. Flags that are not declared still reach the real binary."
        topic: "config"
        task: "maintaining"
        step: 6
        mode: "shell"
        why: "Nushell ships no completion for pass, so this is an extern signature written by hand — which is also why an undeclared flag has to pass through rather than error."
        # `pass` itself is the external binary and nushell reports no handle
        # for it; what this config actually adds is the completion, so that is
        # what the target names. Corrected 2026-08-30 — the old
        # `{kind: "command", name: "pass"}` was stale in both directions at
        # once: it resolved to nothing, and the completer it should have named
        # was reported undocumented.
    }
    # theme switcher (04-shell/09)
    {
        cmd: "theme"
        title: "Pick a colour scheme with a live preview"
        use: "Run `theme` and the scheme catalog opens in television with the current and previous scheme at the top; browsing retints the window background live, `Enter` applies the pick, and `Esc` leaves the theme exactly as it was."
        topic: "config"
        task: "appearance"
        step: 1
        mode: "shell"
        also: ["theme toggle"]
        why: "Browsing never runs `tinty apply` — the preview is one OSC 11 escape, because an apply per focused row would fire tinty's whole hook chain on every keystroke. The one real apply happens after tv exits, in the live shell, so the hooks see the real environment rather than a stripped television-action subprocess."
    }
    {
        cmd: "theme toggle"
        title: "Swap back to the previous scheme"
        use: "Run `theme toggle` — or press `F6` in the terminal, which is bound to it — and the scheme active before the last change comes back, while the one you are leaving becomes the new \"previous\". Run it again and you are back where you started: it is a swap, not a cycle."
        topic: "config"
        task: "appearance"
        step: 2
        does: "switch-scheme"
        mode: "shell"
        also: ["theme"]
    }
    {
        cmd: "llm"
        title: "Pick a model, then the agent to run it"
        use: "Run `llm` on its own and two fuzzy lists follow: first the model — every provider's shelf grouped by provider, `auto` and `auto:free` included, `native:*` for the Max plan — then which agent runs it (Claude Code, pi, whatever agents.json lists). A `native:*` pick narrows the agent list to the agents that have that native; one candidate skips the second list."
        topic: "agents"
        task: "agents"
        step: 2
        mode: "shell"
        also: ["llm claude [model]", "llm status"]
        why: "The model is the decision; the agent is how you talk to it. Asking in that order means the same shelf serves every agent, and adding an agent is an entry in agents.json, not a new launcher."
    }
    {
        cmd: "llm claude [model]"
        title: "Start Claude Code on a chosen model"
        use: "Run `llm claude` with no argument and a picker opens, grouped by provider, each row carrying its tier and what the record says it is good at; pick a row and Claude Code starts on that model. `llm claude <model>` skips the picker. `auto` and `auto:free` let the router pick per request, best for the job first and every other model behind it until one answers; `native:opus` bypasses the proxy and runs on the Max plan. The proxy is started when it is down. The picked model's context window is declared at launch, so auto-compact fires against what the model actually serves. `llm pi <model>` is the same for pi — agents are entries in the router's agents.json, in ~/dev/llm-router."
        topic: "agents"
        task: "agents"
        step: 3
        mode: "shell"
        also: ["llm sync", "llm status"]
        why: "One binary owns the picker, the proxy and the launch, so every shell and nvim's `<leader>xc` get the same one. An earlier split — a nushell skin calling a script — meant a stale shell called a launcher that no longer existed."
    }
    {
        cmd: "llm sync"
        title: "Refresh the shelf: every model, its price, what it can do, how it ranks"
        use: "Run `llm sync` after adding a provider to ~/dev/llm-router/providers.json, or when a provider gains or loses models: it fetches every provider's inventory, prices, capabilities and context windows, the public rankings (Arena per job, OpenRouter usage), folds in our own record, and writes models.json plus the proxy's config. Restart the proxy (`llm serve`) to pick the config up."
        topic: "agents"
        task: "agents"
        step: 4
        mode: "shell"
        also: ["llm claude [model]", "llm status"]
        why: "The routing is derived, never hand-edited — a hand-edited route outlives the model it names and fails at the moment you most want the model."
    }
    {
        cmd: "llm status"
        title: "See which models are parked and who is best at which job"
        use: "Run `llm status` for the models the APIs refused — with the reason in their own words, since when, and when the router tries them again — and the top models per job from the record and the public boards. `llm status clear [model]` puts one, or all, back on the shelf now."
        topic: "agents"
        task: "agents"
        step: 5
        mode: "shell"
        also: ["llm claude [model]", "llm sync"]
        why: "A refused model parks itself from the error text and comes back by itself when the cooldown lapses, so a topped-up balance needs nobody to do anything — this is where you read that state rather than guess it."
    }
    {
        cmd: "pearde [cmd]"
        title: "Work the PRD board this repository plans with"
        use: "Run `pearde` on its own for the board on one page, `pearde help` for the subcommands, `pearde plan` for what runs next. It is a python entry point in ~/dev/infra/pearde, aliased here because the tool installs no binary of its own."
        topic: "agents"
        task: "agents"
        step: 22
        mode: "shell"
        also: ["cc [...args]", "help --json"]
        why: "This alias is half of a pair and does not work alone. Every subcommand that MOVES a PRD stamps who did it, reading `$env.PEARDE_AS` from env.nu, and refuses outright when it is unset — measured 2026-09-02, `pearde sweep --dry` without it answers `refused — persona:` and does nothing. The alias points at the SOURCE repo, never at the `.claude/skills/pearde` symlink inside a project: every install on this machine links into that one repo, so a project-local path would answer only inside that project."
    }
]
